[Music]  time now for morning rounds our look at  the medical news of the week first up a  worrisome rise in the occurrence of  liver cancer it's been increasing since  the mid 1970s and according to a new  report conducted by the American Cancer  Society  liver cancer death rates are rising  faster than any other form of the  disease with the death rate doubling  since the mid 1980s the American Cancer  Society estimates that this year there  will be nearly 41,000 new cases and  close to 29,000 deaths here to talk  about it is CBS news chief medical  correspondent dr. Jon LaPook and CBS  News contributor dr. Tara Narula good  morning to you both  morning Tara did the report give any  reason for the rise in cancer well the  interesting thing about liver cancer is  that 60% of it is attributable to  potentially modifiable risk factors some  of those risk factors have been  increasing over the past few decades  things like type 2 diabetes and obesity  in addition alcohol plays a role we know  that there's a 10% increase in the risk  per drink per day and smoking which we  think of as associated with lung cancer  actually increases the risk of liver  cancer by about 50% and then you have to  think about hepatitis hepatitis B and C  globally hepatitis B is really the  biggest cause of liver cancer in this  country really only the cause of less  than 5% of liver cancers but hepatitis C  very prevalent high numbers in that baby  boomer population those born between  1945 and 1965 they actually count for  about 80 percent of the cases of  hepatitis C in this country dr. LaPook  there were significant disparities in  terms of who was affected by this what  is what can you tell us me yeah and you  know as I'm listening to you I want to  point out to our viewers we are talking  about cancer that starts in the liver  not cancer that starts somewhere else  and spreads to the liver which of course  is deadly but yes there are a lot of  racial disparities ethnic disparities as  with other cancers we're seeing and the  lowest death rate was in whites then  blacks Hispanics Asian and Pacific  Islanders and the greatest death rate  was an American Indians and Alaska  Natives and they're different with more  than twice that of whites and it's felt  largely due to different differences in  these risk factors that Tara was talking  about and to some extent to access to  care Tara did the authors suggest there  was any way to reverse these trends well  it's important for us to look at  because the prognosis for liver cancer  is not great we know that survival of  five years after diagnosis about one in  five so clearly we can affect these  modify loris factors so better control  of diabetes weight control decreased  alcohol avoiding smoking and then when  you look at those hepatitis factors you  want to make sure that people are  vaccinated for hepatitis B which became  part of routine childhood vaccinations  in 1982 and for Hep C do we need to do a  better job in terms of surveillance and  screening the CDC recommends that  everyone born in that baby boomer  generation get tested at least once and  then treatment in 2014 we have new oral  drugs that can treat hepatitis C very  good cure rates low risk low side effect  rates and so that would be important and  then finally looking at populations that  are at high risk and really targeting  those so people who are incarcerated and  those with needle sharing to try to  avoid that as well good words of wisdom  our next topic something we all  experience stomach problems a recently  really serve a by Merck manuals part of  the merck pharmaceutical company looked  at how Americans deal with digestive  discomfort a little over 2,000 American  adults participated the survey found  that nearly a quarter of people  experienced digestive discomfort once a  week or more and only 16% who  experienced digestive discomfort visited  a doctor and the sort of doesn't  surprise me any surprises dr. Luke what  are the other finding surprising well  about 45 percent tried to treat it  themselves with over-the-counter  medicines about 35 percent adjusted  their diet  about 27 percent just dealt with it they  just I guess toughed it out at about 22  percent sought some sort of alternative  medicine some natural remedy Tara what  kind of strategies to people have for  trying to eliminate foods from their guy  well about 18 percent just eliminated  them right on the spot and with the ones  that they thought were contributing  about 47 percent decreased their  exposure to things that they thought  they might be intolerant to 32 percent  conducted their own research and about  13 to 14 percent talked to friends and  family or tried taking medications as  John mention but I think the important  thing is that lots of times just seeing  a doctor and getting tested and going  over your family history and what your  triggers might be is really more  important and can be beneficial than  people just suffering  and trying to diagnose themselves  running to the bathroom as they  mentioned embarrassed you know - to the  bathroom over stretched pants dirty it's  it's something that so many Americans  deal with doc and it's your feel to  steer a your expertise yes I what's I  mean like what's surprising to you in  all of this well what yeah what stood  out was how many people have a  gastrointestinal problem and then how  few people seek the help of a doctor  they said a doctor in the survey or  other clinician I would say can can also  help I am a gastroenterologist I am  biased one of the reasons why you do  need to go to somebody a clinician is  that there are so many different things  that can cause gastrointestinal problems  I mean it could be stress it could be  diet it could be some underlying problem  like inflammatory bowel disease or  celiac disease very often diet if you're  lactose intolerant or some other thing  the way to handle this is to go to  somebody have them sit down and very  carefully slowly go over your diet it's  very tough to figure it out yourself  because there are so many different  factors I take people through their day  what do you have for breakfast what do  you have for lunch and very often  something will come out and it's such an  amazing feeling to be able to  somebody'll come after 20 years and it's  nothing too simple just like you have a  lactose intolerance and so it's one of  the reasons why I love being a GI is you  can really help people if they don't  come to you then it's it's tough to help  them but which is why I like morning  rounds because we can go to that right  all right finally they're often  considered part of the family but along  with companionship can having a dog  improves your health well a new study  published by biomed central look at the  impact of owning a dog for people 65 and  older in the UK the study found owning a  dog led to an average 22 minutes of  additional walking time and dog owners  had significantly less time when they  were City what about cat owners does it  count for me if my wife walks the dog  you points for that  but I would say go get a dog they're  great I love my dog ash little ash  doctor's Jon LaPook Darren a rule always  good to see you thanks for your time

